A||||14||ThatRickGuy||Engine out the top help||||1||
Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||06-23-2000||09:37 PM||||Hey guys, I know this has been talked about, but I'm still a little fussy on it. I was planning on dropping the engine out the bottom, but that ment dropping the transaxle/tranny, cradle, meesing with the rear end needing a new alignment and everything. But now that I have the engine mostly torn apart from the top, I'm wondering, pull the altinator and accesories, pull the 'y'pipe, and some extra little goodies, would it fit out the top? I think I've heard of people doing this befor w/ the 6 cylinder. I remember people saying you had to swing the engin to the right to clear the drive shaft, but is there more? I can see 5 bolts on the bell housing, and my buddy said he saw 2 on the passanger side, how many more are there? I have the top half off the intake, should I pull the rest off also? can I leave the exhaust manifolds on? sorry for the long post, I'm just in need of knowledge. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||06-23-2000||11:37 PM||||Okay, I've been reading some other poeples web pages and I'm getting chased in circles <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  apparently, some paople say you can, others say you can't, I'm hoping for the can's on this one, I'm just not catching the underside removal, and the tranny and all is fine, it's just one wrist pin. But I am doing this operation in a fully stocked auto shop, w/ hoist, chery picker, and plenty of tools. Has anyone here ever done this befor?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fret Naught||06-24-2000||12:33 AM||||Rick guy,<BR>I have never done the Fiero engine before, but if it is any consilation, My Areostar van engine had to be dropped out the bottom. Slickest thing I ever saw/did. impossible without a hoist, basically just four big bolts after all the litte stuff was removed.<BR>It was alot easier than pulling them out the top like I was used to doing.||||reg||1
Z||000003||BN Boomer||06-24-2000||01:02 AM||||Yes, it is possible to pull the engine out the top. Here is mine going back in...<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/boomers_fiero_files/fiero1.jpg"> <P>Personally, having done it both ways now I would recommend dropping the cradle unless you need a rolling chassis while the engine is out. My reason being that accessing everything is sooooooo much easier with the cradle out and it really isn't that much more work to drop the cradle. The only additional work that doing it entails is removing the wheels and brake calipers, strut tower mounts, shifter cable and tranny fluid lines (mine's an auto) and the 4 cradle bolts themselves.<P>Whichever route you choose, good luck. It really ain't all that difficult.||||reg||1
Z||000004||BN Boomer||06-24-2000||01:03 AM||||PS If you have any other questions, don't hesitate to ask.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Zman302||06-24-2000||02:02 AM||||I pulled the engine from the top on mine.  Really wasnt a bad job.  I did every bit of it myself and had the engine out in about 1 1\2 hrs and when installing the new engine i had about the same amount of time in it.  My friend that i got the car from said that they pull all of theirs thru the top so i figured it was the way to go.  Only thing i would maybe see that would be easier would be that the motor could be fully assembled going back in.  I had to add everything after it was in and ran into a tough time with the exh. manifold.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||ThatRickGuy||06-24-2000||07:48 AM||||thanks for the pointers guys. I'm thinking I'll still go for the top. I'm not sure if I'll be able to check this durring the day, but, do the exhaust manifolds need to come off?this is my current worry, I can get the back one, but that front one would be damn near impossible... well, I've gotta get back to the shop, their gonna start charging me lift time pretty fast now.||||reg||1
Z||000007||BN Boomer||06-24-2000||02:10 PM||||No, the exhaust manifolds don't have to come off to pull the engine out the top. I didn't remove either on mine. In fact, I would strongly recommend re-installing the front manifold before putting the new engine back in the car. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||06-24-2000||03:35 PM||||If it's only a wrist pin,then why aren't you just dropping the pan,and pulling the heads?<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000009||ThatRickGuy||06-25-2000||01:29 AM||||Okay guys, thanks for all the pointers, but, I chickened out... I dropped the craddle. took a grand total of 11 hours to drop the craddle, get the engine off and 1/2 striped, and put the tranny back into the car (needed to roll it off the hoist. being my first time ever pulling an engine, I don't think I did half bad. Putting it back in is going to be the hard part, when I was cleaning up I came accross 2 of the wire/tube holders, that bolt into the block... now I have no idea where they go. ahh well, how detailed is the heyes manual? I'm gonna pick one up probrably monday. I figured as long as I'll most likely to get fired from my second job after this week, (calling up saying, hey, I won't be in for a few days cause I'm pulling an engine) I think I spend tomorrow opening up that C channel for my subs, see if they'll fit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I'll keep ya posted if anything changes.||||reg||1
Z||000010||ThatRickGuy||06-25-2000||06:11 PM||||Okay, got back in the shop today, started tearing down the engine, but as soon as I pulled the oil pan it was one big 'Oh Sh*t'er. little alluminum shavings, #6 cylinder has uneaven wear. I was trying to get the heads off to check it from the top, but I guess you have to pull the entire intake to get them off, so I was just about there when it was time to clean up and leave. Just out of curiosity, are the 88 v6s aluminum blocks? didn't they use some sort of sleave on the cylinder? I'm just wondering where the aluminum is coming from.||||reg||1
Z||000011||GT Bastard||06-25-2000||06:56 PM||||Cast iron...||||reg||1
Z||000012||Taijiguy||06-25-2000||11:32 PM||||Interesting that the suspect part was a wrist pin. I have a similar situation. I pulled the topside off mine today to try and remedy an annoying clacking noise that's been present since I rebuilt it a couple of years ago. It hasn't gotten worse, I just got tired of waiting to see if it would make itself more evident. I was hoping to find a crappy lifter, but no such luck. I do have a couple of questions for anyone who may have an answer about that; I measured the play in the pistons with a dial indicator, and it seems that one has about .007 slack someplace in the rod bearing or wrist pin. What would be acceptable here? Based on this information, anyone have any thoughts? Seems to me if it was a bearing that it would have gotten worse over two years. But I don't remember any unusual slack in the piston/rod assembly, piston and pin were new .040 parts. I dunno, looking for answers. Any clues? <BR>(I'm asking because the motor is still in, and the pistons are still in the motor, and I'm trying to decide what to do next)<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 06-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||lowCG||06-26-2000||10:42 AM||||There really shouldn't be any measureable slack there,but how did you measure it?<BR>Fit of the wist pin/piston should be 1/2 to almost two thou.<BR>Fit of the rod/crank should be one to two thou.at the most.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Taijiguy||06-26-2000||11:31 AM||||I measured the play by setting a dial indicator to measure piston travel. Then I turned the crank so that the piston was being pulled down. Then I pressed on top of the piston, that gave me total play of the rotating assembly. Most of the pistons measured between .002-.003 but one showed .007. I thought about a busted piston skirt, but there's NO sign of wear to the cylinder, no scratching or anything. Someone also suggested that since it's been there from the very first day I might have had a thin bearing. (OK I admit it, I didn't plastigage ALL of the bearings, a mistake I'll not repeat again!) ||||reg||1
